---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Flavor Guides",
    tags: [
      "dole whip protein powder",
      "be amazing dole whip protein",
      "dole whip pineapple protein",
      "dole whip strawberry protein",
      "pineapple protein powder",
      "vegan dole whip protein"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Smoothie Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-protein-powder-for-smoothies/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Dole Whip Protein Powder</Fragment>
      Only one brand holds the Dole Whip license for protein powder. This guide covers its two flavors, what the labels contain, what they cost per 25 g, and the pineapple alternatives worth buying instead.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The 4 Picks</a>
      <a href="#official">Is There an Official One?</a>
      <a href="#how-picked">How We Picked</a>
      <a href="#whats-inside">What's in It</a>
      <a href="#dairy-free">Vegan and Dairy-Free?</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#taste">Taste and Texture</a>
      <a href="#recipe">Protein Soft Serve</a>
      <a href="#which-one">Which to Buy</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Be Amazing Dole Whip Pineapple is the only licensed Dole Whip protein powder, with 20 g of vegan protein per scoop at about $2.92 per 25 g at the Amazon price we saw. The strawberry version costs less per gram, and non-licensed pineapple wheys cost less still. Below are four picks, their labels, the cost math and how to blend a protein soft serve.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Dole Whip Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Be Amazing Vegan Protein in Dole Whip Pineapple is the only protein powder sold under the real Dole Whip name, and it gives 20 g of plant protein and 140 calories per scoop, per the label at the time of writing. It costs about $2.92 per 25 g of protein at the 15-serving Amazon price we saw on 20 November 2026. The same brand's Dole Whip Strawberry costs less per gram at about $2.30. Everything else labeled pineapple or tropical is a lookalike, not a licensed Dole Whip product.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the choice is simple. Buy the pineapple if you want the flavor you know from the theme parks. Buy the strawberry if you want the licensed line for less money. If you only want a pineapple protein shake and don't care about the name, Nutricost's pineapple whey costs about $1.61 per 25 g and Ascent's Pineapple Coconut clear whey is the one with an Informed Sport certification.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Licensed Dole Whip?</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein / scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Dairy-free?</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Be Amazing Dole Whip Pineapple</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.92</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Be Amazing Dole Whip Strawberry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.30</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Nutricost Pineapple Whey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No (whey)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.61</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ascent Clear Pineapple Coconut</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No (whey)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.12</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Dole Whip Protein Powders and Closest Alternatives, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two licensed Dole Whip flavors, then two clearly labeled pineapple alternatives that are not Dole products. Ranked on label quality, certification and cost per 25 g of protein. We have not tasted these; taste notes are what buyers report. Prices are the Amazon prices we saw on 20 November 2026 and change often. Label figures are per each brand's panel at the time of writing.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="official" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is There an Official Dole Whip Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes: Be Amazing, the brand also known as BEAM, makes the only licensed Dole Whip protein powder, and it comes in two flavors, Pineapple and Strawberry. Both carry a Dole Food Company trademark line on the label, which is how you can tell a licensed product from a pineapple powder that simply borrows the idea. The Pineapple label we read carries a 2026 Dole copyright, and the Strawberry label a 2025 one.
          </p>

          <p>
            The partnership started with greens, not protein. BEAM first worked with Dole on an Orange Peach Mango flavor of its Super Greens powder, then in 2024 it announced Dole Whip Strawberry Vegan Protein alongside a Dole Whip Pineapple pre-workout, as <a href="https://www.stack3d.com/2024/05/beam-dole-whip-strawberry-pineapple.html" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Stack3d reported at the time</a>. That detail trips people up: for a while the only pineapple Dole Whip product from BEAM was a pre-workout with caffeine, not a protein. Dole Whip Pineapple protein came later and is now listed on both <a href="https://youcanbeam.com/products/beam-be-amazing-vegan-plant-based-protein-powder-supplement" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Be Amazing's own store</a> and Amazon.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are shopping, check the product name before you add to cart. A tub that says Pre-Workout has no meaningful protein and contains stimulants. The protein version says Vegan Protein, lists 20 g of protein on the front, and comes in a stand-up bag rather than a small tub. On Amazon, the pineapple protein we found was the 15-serving bag; Be Amazing's site also lists a 25-serving pineapple bag, but it showed as unavailable when we checked.
          </p>

          <p>
            Why is Dole Whip a natural fit for a vegan brand? The original soft serve sold at Disney parks and by Dole is itself dairy-free, so a pea-based protein version is closer to the real dessert than a whey shake would be. That is the strongest argument for the licensed product over the alternatives below: it matches the dessert's dairy-free character as well as its flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other brands sell pineapple, pineapple cream, pina colada and tropical punch protein powders, and some of them use soft-serve language in their marketing. None of them can use the Dole Whip name. We include two of them as clearly labeled alternatives because the licensed line has only two flavors, a single vegan formula and a higher price per gram than many whey powders.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How We Picked These Dole Whip Protein Powders
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked these four on label facts, certification status and cost per 25 g of protein; we did not taste them. Taste and texture notes come from buyer reviews and are labeled that way. Every nutrition figure is from the brand's own supplement or nutrition facts panel at the time of writing, and every price is the Amazon price we saw on 20 November 2026.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first filter was the name. Only a product with a Dole license can honestly be called Dole Whip protein powder, and only Be Amazing has one, so both of its Dole Whip flavors are on the list. We then searched Amazon for pineapple, pineapple whip, pina colada and tropical protein powders to find the closest alternatives. We kept products from established brands with a published label and dropped anything we could not verify, including listings from unknown sellers with no ingredient list.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the alternatives, we looked for two different readings of the flavor. Dole Whip is creamy soft serve with a strong pineapple note, so the first alternative is a creamy pineapple whey. Some people mainly want the bright pineapple taste in a light drink after training, so the second is a clear whey isolate with a pineapple coconut profile. Isopure also sells a clear whey in Pineapple Orange Banana; we left it off the cards because we could not confirm its current serving count on Amazon, but it is covered in our <a href="/isopure-clear-protein-powder-review/">Isopure Clear review</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cost per 25 g is our standard yardstick because serving sizes differ. The formula is the price divided by the number of 25 g protein units in the bag: servings times protein per serving, divided by 25. A 20 g scoop and a 25 g scoop look the same on a shelf, but they are not the same amount of protein, and this math puts them on one scale.
          </p>

          <p>
            Certification counts because protein powders are supplements and are not approved by the FDA before sale. A named program such as Informed Sport or NSF Certified for Sport tests for banned substances and publishes a searchable list. A general "third-party tested" claim is better than nothing, but it doesn't tell you who tested what, so we report it as the brand's own claim.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="whats-inside" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Is in Be Amazing Dole Whip Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Be Amazing Dole Whip Pineapple contains 20 g of protein from pea and pumpkin seed protein in a 37.2 g scoop, with 140 calories, 3 g of fat, 8 g of carbohydrate, 4 g of fiber, under 1 g of sugar and 350 mg of sodium, per the label at the time of writing. Each scoop also lists 230 mg of calcium and 4 mg of iron. There are 15 scoops in the bag on Amazon.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ingredient list, in order, is PURIS pea protein, acacia fiber, pineapple juice powder, natural flavors, pumpkin seed protein (sold as Smooth Protein), tapioca maltodextrin, MCT oil on acacia fiber, Himalayan pink salt, trace minerals from red algae (Aquamin), apple fiber, turmeric extract for color, blueberry fiber, tartaric acid, Reb-M and monk fruit extract. The pineapple juice powder ranks above the natural flavors, which is a good sign that real fruit contributes to the taste rather than flavoring alone.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How does the Strawberry label differ?</h3>

          <p>
            Dole Whip Strawberry is almost the same formula with the fruit swapped. Per its 25-serving label, a 35.8 g scoop gives 20 g of protein, 140 calories, 7 g of carbs, 4 g of fiber, under 1 g of sugar and 370 mg of sodium. It uses strawberry juice powder and beet root powder for color, and its sweeteners are steviol glycosides (Reb-M) and monk fruit. Iron is a little lower at 3 mg per scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two numbers deserve attention. First, 20 g of protein from a 37 g scoop means only about 54% of the powder is protein; the rest is fiber, fat, flavor and minerals that create the creamy texture. A whey isolate like the Ascent manages 20 g from 26 g. Second, the sodium is high for a protein powder. Two scoops a day add 700 mg of sodium, close to a third of the 2,300 mg daily limit the Dietary Guidelines suggest for most adults. If you have high blood pressure or kidney disease, check with your doctor or a registered dietitian before making it a daily habit.
          </p>

          <p>
            On sweeteners, the line avoids sucralose, acesulfame potassium and added sugar, relying on stevia and monk fruit. That suits people who avoid artificial sweeteners, though stevia has its own aftertaste for some. If you avoid stevia specifically, see our list of <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-stevia/">protein powders without stevia</a>; if you avoid sucralose, the <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">no-artificial-sweetener guide</a> is the better starting point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dairy-free" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Dole Whip Protein Powder Vegan and Dairy-Free?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes: both Be Amazing Dole Whip flavors are vegan and made without dairy, using pea and pumpkin seed protein, and the brand describes them as gluten-free and soy-free. That matches the original Dole Whip, which is a dairy-free soft serve. The two alternatives on this page are not dairy-free: Nutricost's pineapple powder is whey concentrate, and Ascent's Pineapple Coconut is whey isolate. Both labels list milk.
          </p>

          <p>
            If lactose is your only issue, the Ascent isolate may still work for you, because isolates are filtered to remove most of the lactose. Concentrate keeps more of it, so Nutricost is the riskier choice for lactose-sensitive stomachs. Our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein powder guide</a> explains the difference. If you have a true milk allergy, skip both whey options entirely and stay with the Be Amazing line or another product from our <a href="/best-dairy-free-protein-powder/">dairy-free protein powder picks</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pea protein has a reputation for being gritty and earthy, which is why Be Amazing leans on a patented pea protein and a pumpkin seed protein designed for a smoother feel, plus MCT oil and fiber for body. Buyers' opinions on whether that works are mixed, which we cover below. For a broader look at plant blends and how their amino acids compare with whey, see our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">plant-based protein powder guide</a> and <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allergy note: the Ascent label states that it is produced on equipment that also processes tree nuts and peanuts. We did not find a similar shared-equipment statement on the Be Amazing panel we read, but that is not proof there is none. Anyone with a serious food allergy should read the physical bag and contact the brand before buying, and pregnant readers or anyone on medication should run a new supplement past their doctor.
          </p>
        </div>

